{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested for the R1 evidence-only design at exact 665e490bf474d44065ad6618dce4478c76306623, invitation 6d348d8a and my promisec6cce2b6. Main artifactf47508dc first, all three current subjects reviewed. Normal evidence-only prepare binds to original requestc69b573d and confers no implementation closure. The identity and relationship rules below need correction before this contract is adopted. The complete functional scope remains owed.\n\nP1 — Distinguish incoming delivery occurrences when deriving child scope identities. Main note sections 2.1 and 7.2, lines107–109 and 845, define a handler-created child's cause as the digest of Message alone. Section 7.4 instead identifies a request by its source FactRef and send ordinal. Consider S.5/0 and S.6/0 sending identical tell-message bytes to creatorC. Both verified deliveries independently ask their handler to create the same kind/definition at outgoing creation ordinal0. Seed contains the same creator, message digest and ordinal, so the two distinct operations produce the same ScopeId without a hash collision. The child genesis and result refer to only the first creating request; the second cannot obtain its own correctly correlated result under 7.4's source/ordinal checks. This breaks separate creation and causal settlement.\n\nDefine a distinct canonical delivery-cause byte domain over the authenticated incoming source fact, incoming send ordinal and message digest, or an equally complete immutable delivery identity. A retransmission of that exact delivery must keep its seed; a distinct delivery must receive a distinct seed. Update seed derivation, child checks, genesis replay and the correction matrix together. Keep the causing foreign fact already sealed and avoid introducing the creating entry's own hash or speculative local sequence into its bytes. A compact two-distinct-requests/same-message table and exact retry table are sufficient design witnesses; no runtime probe or new test quota is requested.\n\nP2 — Define one relationship state for multiple sends from the same owner entry. Sections6.4/6.6 and 7.3, lines532/541 and 862–869, permit several sends but use owner seq alone as the relationship revision. The conflicting-effect check at513–515 applies to effects, and no corresponding rule forbids two relate sends to the same target, owner item and relationship name. If one entry sends set and removed for that same key, both revisions are equal. The first delivery applies; the other is superseded. The relationship's state is then decided by transport arrival order rather than a uniquely ordered owner history.\n\nThe smallest repair is to refuse duplicate resolved relationship keys among one entry's sends, including aliases through fields/slots/self. Alternatively specify revision comparison over an ordered seq/send-ordinal pair and update source, receiver, result and replay rules consistently. State the chosen rule in validation and the wire meaning, and show both delivery orders. Preserve out-of-order updates from distinct entries, exact result correlation and close/reopen deduplication.\n\nP2 — Specify the timed-transition commit path. Section 5.1 includes timed inputs among all writers using the same protocol, while5.2 first applies every due transition before snapshot and stops a commit when any transition is due. It does not say how the selected timed input avoids encountering itself in that drain/restart rule. As written, the universal protocol admits a recursive drain or perpetual restart precisely when an expiry must advance the head. This is a static design ambiguity, not an executed deadlock.\n\nSpecify an ordered drain whose selected timed input revalidates its own item/deadline, local head and one real clock reading in its transaction, with the appropriate exception for that selected transition. Define simultaneous-deadline order, restart behavior and clock-behind behavior without letting an ordinary act bypass a newly due expiry. The intended atomic local validation and expiry fencing should remain unchanged.\n\nAdditional bounded-read clarification, owned with R4. ItemType.max at483–486 bounds live items, while range guards at597–606 can include final states and 9.1 at1173 promises a one-response summary because item counts are bounded by the definition. Repeated open/final cycles satisfy the live limit while accumulating retained final items. Specify the items a summary/range guard covers and its total retained-item/work/response bound, or pagination and indexed complete evaluation. An incomplete scan cannot establish none or a complete count. R4's openly pending budgets may provide the eventual bound; a live-item maximum alone does not prove it. Do not invent a numerical capacity target or delete retained duty/provenance data as a shortcut.\n\nKnown correction of record. I read complete builder errata 0bcdeefc and planner acknowledgment 49728940. They explicitly correct the unchanged Genesis replay row and missing advisory row, creation examples that incorrectly named an incarnation, provisional/refused repeat exceptions, and trusted complete dispatch accounting. Those are acknowledged corrections, not additional newly discovered findings. The frozen main/correction matrix still contain those inconsistencies; carry the recorded corrections into the next owned revision so the exact reviewed bytes and replay tables agree. In particular, the new delivery-cause and same-entry relationship findings are not repaired by these errata.\n\nArchitecture. Local ordered scopes, four distinct reference classes, pinned meanings, complete local head validation and the separation of immutable preparation from clock/authority judgment form a coherent direction. Source-owned links, one-item opening identity, alias refusal, exact selected contribution provenance and recursive replay/explicit caller anchors avoid several earlier ambiguities. The complete28 absence/range guard map and 154-row admission inventory are useful source evidence. It openly limits completeness to the modules read and lists policy/evaluator/vocabulary, artifact-adapter and other source omissions. This review does not turn that disclosed partial audit into an exhaustive new evaluator or source proof.\n\nSecurity. The current grant and party guard are both required, links do not grant authority, and a retained fact is not proof of current permission. Runtime namespace/source trust is separately stated from independent replay and foreign entry bytes alone do not prove source admission. The signed intent/accepted-result settlement distinction preserves original intent after revocation without admitting a new act. Unknown delivery/effect outcomes retain correlations, resources, pins and fences. Fixing child identity and relationship order is necessary to preserve that exact provenance. The child-confirmation scheme must also remain consistent with R3's actual ownership/founding fences: a provisional child's permitted sends should be explicit before anyone relies on the erratum's statement that it had done nothing.\n\nSimplification. Resolve these rules in the single seed/entry/relationship/admission contract. Use the existing source/ordinal correlation rather than adding reconciliation exceptions for colliding child names. Rejecting duplicate relationship sends is a small option that preserves scalar revisions. Specify timed drain once for all scope writers. Preserve useful compact invariants and real boundary evidence; do not revive obsolete mutation matrices, old readers/engines/adapters or per-scenario whole-repository gates. All fixes are design text and shared-meaning work, not authorized source implementation.\n\nCompleteness and adoption limits. Section 6.9's platform definitions/capabilities, section 6.10's twelve required lane forms and their replay rules, R2's definition bounds, R3 current authority/founding/hold/publication rules, and R4 settlement reserve/retention/capacity remain pending dependencies. The three illustrated issue-before-hold, selected-contribution and causal-replay witnesses do not make the full R2 application executable. The D1/D5 contract, all three browser outcomes, all-component replacement/removal maps and complete manual/public-package/deployed acceptance remain required. This report neither adopts unresolved choices nor narrows them because of the sprint schedule.\n\nIndependent evidence. I read every byte of the three exact documents: main 92,730 bytes/1,563 lines, SHA256 338cf86f298ffc5d7c0815bbba67048da62e7146adfabde01bb5c4fa54475f67; corrections 13,762 bytes/63 lines, SHA256b9680aa7b9e32184473ffcb073ccf1a93524095a044a581b1005a4466c6e8a81; inventory 88,015 bytes/420 lines, SHA256a2b8f683ff81d48d3136d8a484da1dacc26f858709223c5b2bf6d75a5da7e26f. All hashes match the invitation. I independently checked raw artifact payload head/path, Git blobs and sole raw Rests-On promise 8d614272 for each subject. The isolated registered checkout is clean at the exact head. The original R1 conditions and full frozen017/018 planning context were read.\n\nTwo user-authorized parallel static audits assisted the reference and dependency checks. I independently read all three documents and checked the reported triggers against the contract. Their source spot checks remain attributed static assistance, not my execution evidence. No tests, install, typecheck, build, private diagnostic, probe, repository-code execution, credential/provider effect, deployment or person contact was performed. The previously rejected diagnostic was not rerun, rewritten, extended or delegated, and no reviewer/task/model/backend was replaced.\n\nDisposition. Revise the R1 design and its correction history, retain the same complete commissioning, and publish exact current evidence for a new ordinary independent review. Keep R2/R3/R4 contract agreement and the original S1 personal-commitment reconciliation separately owned. No source implementation or adoption is authorized by a changes-requested evidence-only review.\n\nMandatory head news. I read all five complete records required by the normal review guard: bb51bf8c,36115bc5,20ce53a3,0bcdeefc and49728940. These include the builder's filed-revision account, the complete R2 six-group correction, R1 consistency reading and native-task observation, the full errata and acknowledgment. Their current metadata is stale through historical superseded bases; acknowledging them records that history and can make this normal verdict inherit staleness. I have neither omitted required acknowledgments nor asserted this is a current adoption/landing approval. The three R1 source subjects, original request and assigned invitation themselves remain current. The source-qualified new findings above concern those unchanged exact bytes and will also be recorded on their current direct bases for action.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:f47508dcee3ca7ad71013c75643ce5386a5c73a9","binding":"evidence-only","head":"665e490bf474d44065ad6618dce4478c76306623","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:bb51bf8c225159ddd5ad12f596907f54e68d9183\",\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:36115bc5011ed8396c031b9b35aee26e3a653a48\",\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:20ce53a34eaf73732a245d27c645f4f4a1b30236\",\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:0bcdeefc787284769771d0138675be77b79d9b77\",\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:497289403c55b30551a8af43df6f0ac8902a5994\",\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:c6cce2b699d7a6655d7203d988a7c6bc24a74c55\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:474a5e3d5f855fe5bd05e784f07c722bf02e8886","review_path":"reviewguard@1","stale_bases":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:0bcdeefc787284769771d0138675be77b79d9b77, git: